<article class="prose">
  <h1>NPRPC documentation</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Reference for every public declaration in the C++ headers, the Swift package
    and the IDL, generated from their <code>///</code> comments, plus the
    hand-written guides.
  </p>

  <h2>Why NPRPC exists</h2>
  <p>
    In 2014 I was an industrial automation engineer at a marine oil terminal
    run on Emerson's DeltaV DCS. Working with that system every day made me
    want to build my own, smaller, for home automation — and a distributed
    control system needs RPC of some sort.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first version sat on DCOM, which was fine until I wanted it off
    Windows. Then omniORB, which was buggy in the way that ruins a weekend:
    its thread pool would simply stop spawning threads for new connections.
    In 2020 I watched Bjarne Stroustrup's
    <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ERzENfQ51Ck">“The Beauty and
    Power of ‘Primitive’ C++”</a> at CppCon, where he writes zero-copy
    flat-buffer serialization, and it looked simple enough that writing my own
    RPC stopped sounding crazy.
  </p>
  <p>
    gRPC was the obvious alternative and I did not want it. HTTP/2 is a fine
    protocol for services behind a load balancer, but that is not what I was
    building: I wanted zero-copy messages rather than protobuf parsing, calls
    that need no reply, and eventually a transport that never touches a
    socket. gRPC has none of those, and it is a large dependency to take on
    for their absence. So the first NPRPC was TCP with its own framing and
    not much else. QUIC, an HTTP/3 server, WebSocket, shared memory,
    TypeScript and Swift all came later.
  </p>
  <p>
    Shared memory is the transport that turned out to matter most. It is what
    the <a href="https://lavaui.nikitapn.com">Lava</a> compositor and its apps
    talk over: a round trip takes about 7&nbsp;µs, and calls can be
    fire-and-forget, with no reply to wait for. One IDL file generates both
    the compositor's C++ servant and the apps' Swift client, so the two sides
    cannot drift apart.
  </p>
</article>

